$b['A dogs head pokes out of a tent flap on a camping trip ears flopped and eyes bright. Illustrative linework keeps the tent simple and the face expressive'] = 'Two panels side by side on one image, divided down the middle. LEFT PANEL: the tattoo design alone on a plain white background, shown flat exactly as described, no body, drawn large so it fills most of the panel. RIGHT PANEL: Studio photograph of a real person''s body showing the same exact tattoo where described, healed tattoo on real skin in soft even light, the rest of the body modestly covered by simple plain clothing, only the tattooed area bare, plain seamless light grey studio backdrop - no room, no furniture, no scenery. The tattoo in both panels is identical. The tattoo: A dogs head pokes out of a tent flap on a camping trip, ears flopped and eyes bright. Illustrative linework keeps the tent simple and the face expressive. Adventurous, cozy, fun. In the right panel it is on a man, inner forearm. Create exceptional contemporary tattoo inspiration centered on a specific beloved pet. PETS = WHO I LOVE. The emotional subject is not a generic species. It is one individual animal. The example animal shown in each concept is only an example. NEVER LET THE EXAMPLE PET BECOME THE DESIGN. THE DESIGN MUST STILL WORK WHEN THE CUSTOMER REPLACES THE EXAMPLE WITH THEIR OWN PET. If the concept shows a Doberman, the underlying artistic idea should still work with a Golden Retriever, mixed-breed dog, cat, rabbit, horse, bird or another companion animal whenever logically appropriate. Rotate pets continuously. Do not create a catalog dominated by one or two popular breeds. Include a broad natural mix of dogs, cats, horses, rabbits, birds, reptiles, small mammals and unusual companion animals. Within dogs, rotate breeds, sizes, coat types, ages and appearances. Within cats, rotate coat patterns, breeds, body types and ages. Include mixed-breed animals. Include senior animals. Include young animals. Include rescued ani