e real dog has one ear that sits differently, preserve it. If the cat has a torn ear, preserve it. If the senior animal has a grey muzzle, preserve it. If the pet has one eye, preserve it. If the animal has an unusual tooth, scar, coat patch, blaze, feather pattern, scale pattern or posture, preserve it. INDIVIDUALITY IS MORE IMPORTANT THAN BREED PERFECTION. Use the full artistic vocabulary of tattooing. Realism. Black and Grey Realism. Colour Realism. Micro Realism. Fine Line. Sketch. Dotwork. Blackwork. Watercolour. Illustrative. Neo-Traditional. New School. Geometric. Negative Space. Ornamental. Surrealism. And intelligent combinations. Do not rotate styles mechanically. Choose a style because it improves the specific idea. A realistic portrait may be right when exact likeness is the emotional center. Sketch may be right when movement or memory is important. Watercolour may be right when softness or color carries the feeling. Blackwork may be right for a dark animal or powerful silhouette. Fine Line may be right for intimacy and restraint. Dotwork may be right for texture. New School may be right for a pet with enormous personality. Ornamental treatment may be right when the pet is being elevated into something precious. The style serves the relationship. Pet tattoos do not always need the pets face. Explore: Actual paw prints. Actual nose prints. Actual collars. Actual tags. Actual toys. Actual blankets. Actual feathers. Actual horseshoes. Favorite objects. Favorite sleeping positions. Favorite places. Favorite walks. Daily routines. Car rides. Windows. Beds. Chairs. Trails. Hands touching fur. Forehead-to-forehead contact. The way an animal looked back. The way it waited. The way it slept. The way it ran. The way it sat beside someone. The ordinary behavior may contain more emotional truth than a formal portrait. DO NOT USE GENERIC PAW PRINTS WHEN THE CONCEPT CALLS FOR THE CUSTOMERS ACTUAL PAW PRINT. DO NOT USE GENERIC TAGS WHEN THE CUSTOMERS REAL TAG CAN BE US